## Weigh everything together

No single source is authoritative — piece the whole story together from ALL of the above.
Different facts surface in different places: a false-positive vs a genuine miss is often clearest
from the fix patch + tests; a revert/softening or the reason for it often only appears in the
discussion or changelog; the descriptions frame intent. Synthesize, then extract the two facts.

## Fact 1 — eligibility: is PR2 a genuine, anticipatable missed-edge-case?

- "clean"         — PR2 fixes a genuine BUG: an edge case of PR1's behavior that produced a defect (wrong output, crash, silent wrong result) on some input. A real user-facing defect an agent could have anticipated. This is the only KEEP class. SECURITY fixes count as clean: a change that closes an attack (weak/predictable randomness, injection, XSS, path traversal, auth/authorization bypass, CRLF/header injection, SSRF, ReDoS/DoS) is a real bug even if there is NO observable defect for legitimate users — security bugs are silent to the victim.
- "not_a_bug"     — no user-facing defect AND not a security fix: pure refactor, rename/alias, docstring/docs, deprecation bookkeeping, internal hygiene, type-only change. (Say what non-bug purpose it served.) NB: "no observable defect for legitimate users" does NOT imply not_a_bug — if the change closes an attack, it is `clean` (see above).
- "new_feature"   — PR2 ADDS new capability rather than fixing a defect in existing behavior; the held-out test exercises a NEW API/feature, not a missed edge case of what PR1 did.
- "unpredictable" — PR2 REVERTS or SOFTENS a change PR1 made INTENTIONALLY, for SOCIAL/POLICY reasons (community pushback, "too disruptive", deprecation timing) — not an objective technical defect. An agent cannot foresee a maintainer's later change of heart; the reversal reason usually appears in the discussion or changelog. (BUT: if PR2 reverts because PR1's intentional change had an OBJECTIVE defect visible from spec/code — e.g. its stricter validation rejected valid inputs — that is `clean`, not unpredictable.)
- "weak"          — a real but trivial/borderline defect, or you are genuinely unsure it is a meaningful edge case.

## Fact 2 — task validity: are PR2's held-out tests a well-formed, FAIR task?

"A" if none of the issues below apply. Otherwise the FIRST applicable Bxx code. These flag when passing the held-out tests requires more (or other) than solving the stated edge case — read the test patch closely.

| code | issue | signal |
|------|-------|--------|
| B1  | TEST_SUITE_COUPLING | tests span unrelated modules; a correct fix could fail on unrelated regressions |
| B2  | IMPLICIT_NAMING     | tests assert specific names / signatures / error-message strings / output values that come from the patch, not derivable from the problem |
| B3  | EXTERNAL_DEPENDENCY | essential info lives in external URLs not quoted in the issue/PR |
| B4  | AMBIGUOUS_SPEC      | missing expected behavior, repro, or acceptance criteria |
| B5  | PATCH_ARTIFACTS     | the fix patch bundles UNRELATED changes that the held-out tests nonetheless require |
| B6  | IMPLICIT_KNOWLEDGE  | tests assert a specific approach/internals not stated or inferrable from the repo |
| B7  | INLINE_TEST         | a test is inlined in the fix (source) patch rather than the test patch |
| B8  | VISUAL_REQUIREMENT  | task needs images/screenshots/visual context (incl. markdown images) not readable as text |
| B9  | NON_ENGLISH_TEXT    | the problem statement is partially/fully non-English |
| B10 | TEXT_FILE_TESTS     | the test patch only changes plain-text fixtures (.txt/.csv/…), not code tests |
| B11 | MULTI_ISSUE_PR      | PR2 fixes multiple independent issues; ambiguous which change the tests require |

